  "worked_solution": "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we try to simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =2(tx)^{3}+3(ty)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}{x}^{3}+3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}g(x,y)={t}^{n}(2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2})\n$$\n\nand so $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
